# pots v1.4 — NEGATIVE. A supine heart rate that carries no rdf:type.
#
# WHY THIS FIXTURE IS WORTH MORE THAN ITS OWN CONSTRAINT. pots:supineHeartRate
# is bound with sh:class pots:HeartRateMeasurement at sh:Violation, and sh:class
# reaches only a node carrying rdf:type. The node below is structurally complete
# -- it has the fhir:valueQuantity, the datatype is right, the timing property is
# present and correct -- and it is rejected anyway, for the single reason that
# nobody wrote `a pots:HeartRateMeasurement` on it.
#
# THE VALUE IS 350.0 bpm, WHICH IS THE POINT AND NOT AN OVERSIGHT. It is the same
# out-of-range value heartrate-above-range.INVALID.ttl carries, and that fixture
# reports a MaxInclusiveConstraintComponent for it. Here the identical number
# reports nothing, because the only difference between the two nodes is the
# rdf:type. That makes the two files a controlled pair: same value, typed node
# fails the range check, untyped node is never asked. An in-range value such as
# 72.0 could not state this -- the absence of a range violation would be equally
# explained by the number being legal, so the output would be consistent with the
# shape having fired and passed, and a future spec change that gave
# HeartRateMeasurementShape a target reaching untyped nodes would leave this
# fixture passing unchanged while its header quietly became false.
#
# The record is therefore NOT clinically coherent, unlike every other fixture in
# this directory: a 350 bpm supine baseline against a 118 bpm standing rate does
# not describe a real check, and pots:potsThresholdMet true is arbitrary here.
# That is the cost of making the silence observable, and it is worth paying in
# this one fixture because the silence is the entire claim.
#
# THAT IS THE QUESTION jayostis/spec#14 IS BLOCKED ON, demonstrated where it can
# be demonstrated. That issue wants to replace seventeen `sh:node` sites in
# checkup:IntakeFormDataShape with `sh:class`, relying on each sub-shape's own
# sh:targetClass to validate the nested node at its own severities. The premise
# is that intake sub-records carry rdf:type in real data. If they are bare blank
# nodes, the change silently stops validating them altogether -- and this fixture
# is what that silence looks like from the other side of the same mechanism: the
# untyped node is not merely unvalidated by pots:HeartRateMeasurementShape, it is
# invisible to it. Here a second constraint happens to catch the omission. In
# checkup, after the sh:node is dropped, nothing would.
#
# This is evidence about the MECHANISM, not about checkup's data, and it does not
# unblock spec#14 -- only a look at what CheckupSerializer actually emits can do
# that. It does show, cheaply and in a vocabulary that already ships, that
# "typed or untyped" is a verdict-changing distinction rather than a stylistic one.
#
# EXACTLY ONE CONSTRAINT FIRES:
#   pots:POTSCheckResultShape / path pots:supineHeartRate
#   / ClassConstraintComponent
#
# Note what does NOT fire, and why that is the whole point: no constraint from
# pots:HeartRateMeasurementShape appears in the report at all. That shape reaches
# its focus nodes by sh:targetClass, the node has no type, so the shape never
# sees it. Every constraint it declares is skipped in silence -- including the
# 20-300 bpm range that this node's own 350.0 bpm plainly breaks, and which
# heartrate-above-range.INVALID.ttl reports for that same value on a typed node.
# The missing MaxInclusiveConstraintComponent is the observable evidence: it is
# absent from the one-line report above, and running the pair shows the contrast.
#
# The other four Violation constraints on the check result are satisfied, so the
# untyped reference is the only thing the validator objects to. It is not the
# only thing wrong with the record -- the 350.0 is wrong too, and is ignored,
# which is precisely the finding.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix pots: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/pots/v1#> .
@prefix fhir: <http://hl7.org/fhir/> .
@prefix loinc: <http://loinc.org/rdf#> .
@prefix ucum: <http://unitsofmeasure.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:b0750011-0000-4a00-8000-000000000011> a pots:POTSCheckResult ;
    pots:date "2031-03-14T09:12:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    pots:protocol "nasaLean" ;
    pots:potsThresholdMet true ;
    pots:supineHeartRate <urn:uuid:b0750011-0000-4a00-8000-000000001040> ;
    pots:standingHeartRates (
        <urn:uuid:b0750011-0000-4a00-8000-000000001041>
    ) ;
    cascade:dataProvenance cascade:PatientReported ;
    cascade:schemaVersion "1.4" .

# NO rdf:type, and the value is out of range. A typed node carrying 350.0 reports
# a range violation; this one reports nothing at all. See the header.
<urn:uuid:b0750011-0000-4a00-8000-000000001040>
    fhir:code loinc:8867-4 ;
    fhir:valueQuantity [
        fhir:value "350.0"^^xsd:double ;
        fhir:unit "beats/minute" ;
        fhir:code ucum:min-1
    ] ;
    pots:secondsSinceStand "0.0"^^xsd:double .

<urn:uuid:b0750011-0000-4a00-8000-000000001041> a pots:HeartRateMeasurement ;
    fhir:code loinc:8867-4 ;
    fhir:valueQuantity [
        fhir:value "118.0"^^xsd:double ;
        fhir:unit "beats/minute" ;
        fhir:code ucum:min-1
    ] ;
    pots:secondsSinceStand "600.0"^^xsd:double .
